Translation patch: `Sid::deserialize_tag` checks the tag bytewise

Change: In `Sid::deserialize_tag` (patch 71), `match Tag::new(tag_str.to_owned()) { Ok(tag) => Ok(Some(tag)), Err(e) => Err(io_error_from(InvalidData, e)) }` becomes `if tag_bytes_valid(tag_bytes) { Ok(Some(Tag(Cow::Owned(tag_str.to_owned())))) } else { Err(misc::trusted::sid_tag_invalid_error(tag_str)) }`. `tag_bytes_valid` (with its recursive helper `tag_bytes_valid_from`) is a new private function checking the bytes: 1 to `Tag::MAX_LEN` bytes, the first in `a-z` or `_`, the others in `a-z`, `0-9` or `_`. `sid_tag_invalid_error` is a new function of the module of patch 64 whose body builds the old error: `io_error_from(InvalidData, e)` of the `Err(e)` of `Tag::new(tag_str.to_owned())`.
Why: `Tag::validate` walks the `char`s of the string (`str::chars`), and Aeneas' `Chars::next` is an opaque function without a model, so whether a tag read from a `COM_BINLOG_DUMP_GTID` packet is accepted could not be stated. `Tag::new` itself (public, with its `InvalidTag` payload) is unchanged.
Behaviour: Identical. `tag_str` is the `&str` of `tag_bytes` (checked UTF-8, `sid_tag_from_utf8`). `Tag::validate` accepts it iff it has 1 to 32 bytes (`str::len` counts bytes), its first `char` is in `a-z` or `_` and the others in `a-z`, `0-9` or `_`; all these `char`s are ASCII, so this holds iff the bytes satisfy the same conditions, which is `tag_bytes_valid` (a non-ASCII `char` is encoded by bytes `>= 0x80`, rejected bytewise). On acceptance the tag is `Tag(Cow::Owned(tag_str.to_owned()))`, the value `Tag::new` returns for a `String`. On rejection `Tag::new` returns `Err`, so `sid_tag_invalid_error` returns the same `io::Error` as before (its `unreachable!` is never reached). The buffer is skipped before the check, as before.

